1	// THE FAKE GITHUB — the in-memory repository the whole harness runs against.
2	//
3	// It stands in for `src/world/github.mjs`: `port` carries exactly that module's
4	// names, implemented over the store below, and the scenario levers hang off the
5	// harness object beside it (see `clock.mjs` for why the two are separate).
6	//
7	// TWO SURFACES, ONE STORE. The port's named operations read and write the store
8	// directly. `port.makeGh` — and `restCall`, the same call for a caller holding a
9	// token — return a TRANSPORT that routes a REST path onto those same store
10	// primitives, because the stages under `src/` still thread `gh` through and call
11	// the real operations over it. Neither surface is a copy of the other: both are
12	// thin over `issue()`, `addLabelTo()`, `postComment()` and friends, so a
13	// behaviour modelled once is modelled for both. The router is what retires when
14	// the stages take a port object instead of a transport.
15	//
16	// WHAT IT MODELS, because these are the limitations the mechanism is built
17	// around and a fake without them proves the easy half:
18	//
19	//  - COMMENT IDS ARE SERVER-ASSIGNED AND STRICTLY INCREASING, and comment
20	//    timestamps have ONE-SECOND granularity. That pair is the whole reason claim
21	//    arbitration orders by id and never by time.
22	//  - LABELS ARE DEFINED OBJECTS. Applying a name the repo does not define 422s
23	//    (behind `unknownLabels422()`, see below), which is why `ensureLabels` runs
24	//    before anything is assigned.
25	//  - LABEL WRITES ARE GRANULAR AND A SWAP IS NOT ATOMIC. `tearNextLabelSwap()`
26	//    lands the remove and fails the add, leaving the item wearing no status —
27	//    the one thing a torn swap can actually do.
28	//
29	// FAULTS ARE OFF BY DEFAULT and switched on per scenario. Every one of them is a
30	// thing GitHub does to a healthy caller, so a scenario turns one on to say "and
31	// now the platform misbehaves", never to describe our own code.
32	
33	const PERM_NONE = 'none';
34	
35	// A comment's `created_at`, at GitHub's own one-second resolution.
36	const secondIso = (ms) => new Date(Math.floor(ms / 1000) * 1000).toISOString();
37	
38	const q = (path) => {
39	  const i = path.indexOf('?');
40	  return i === -1 ? new URLSearchParams() : new URLSearchParams(path.slice(i + 1));
41	};
